// WAX GLOVES
:entry
* In 1834, the first angel was discovered.
* They found it in a cave in the Russian countryside.
* Like the others, it had no name.
* Like the others, it made an offer.
angel -- Enter my pact, and I shall give you my power.
* Two thousand five hundred years have passed. The world is different.
* The angel is gone. In its place lies immense caverns, unnaturally dark.
* They say there's a city in the blackness. They say there are treasures and secret knowledge.
* They say the angel is still there, deep in the caves.
* Many are going, for many reasons. You are one of them.
[scene: cave]
* You and your guide tread down what might have been a stairway, centuries ago.
* The torches, aided by magic, drip warm Wax across your fingers.
myra -- Art thou [explore: affrighted]? Thine idle legs shall make the journey slow.
> The torches are going dim.
	myra -- As is their course. The caverns layer darkness upon itself, folding it like a veil.
		Soon they shall scarcely light our feet.
	> We won't make /any/ progress if they go out.
> The path is narrow.
	myra -- Better shoes would do thee good.
	> My shoes are fine. We just need to tread carefully.
	> They're a bit worn, but 
> There could be others ahead.
	myra -- Aye. There shall be.
[forget: affrighted]
[exit]

:myra_face
* Myra the chant-singer.
* Her face is more animal than human, changed by magic.
> [skip] (It's disgusting.)
	* She and her cohorts live and die by ancient traditions and artifacts, but the human form seems not among those precious things.
	* They're free to contort and ruin themselves for shallow utility and aesthetic, leaving their own species behind.
	* Hypocrites. The ugliness of their meddling is the burden they deserve.
> [skip] (It's beautiful.)
	* She's sculpted herself into what she wants to be. Isn't that what we all want?
[back]

:affrighted
you -- “Affrighted?”
myra -- Thou dost refute the charge?
> I'm just confused by your phrasing. It's hard to understand what you're saying, at times.
	myra -- I understand. We learned from the echos of [explore: the ancient songs | ancient_songs]. Those deaf to it are force to relearn, again and again, and lose yet more of their memory.
> I do.
	myra -- Then perhaps thou shouldst walk faster.
[back]

:ancient_songs
> What are the ancient chants? Who sang them?
	myra -- 
> How do you hear these songs?
[back]